In the Take Back Programs, companies should be able to develop collaborative programs with the customers. The aim of this goal is to build a strong relationship in order to get back the product. Also, this help the companies to  build customer loyalty schemes, convenient to both customers and companies. To develop a good understanding of drivers of return flow characteristics the designers can based their decision on data-based analysis by understand product category, life-cycle lenght, market value of used product. customer segment, ease of return, rebate policy, etc (Toktay et al. 2006). Different schemes can be use:

- Df Buy-Back Model
In order to collect the product in good condition to be reused, a possible strategy is to buy-back the product. This strategy implies that the quality of the product must be worth enough to be buy-back. The managing of various qualities of the product can complicate the selection process.

- Df Deposit-Refund Model
Here, the product cost is subject to a tax. When the item is returned, customers can be refunded. The amount, time, and potentially performance of the returned product can be associated with the return of the money. To improve collection rates of products, the minimum deposit/refund requirement should be higher than the value of the product. 

- Df Service Model
Here, the company sell the service of the product and not the product. In this way, the company is able to maintain the full control of the product through the various contracts typologies between B2C and B2B.